{"name":"Surfer hair","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Surfer_hair.jpg","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surfer_hair.jpg","caption":"Surfer hair","source":"Wikimedia Commons via Wikipedia"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Surfer hair is a medium to long, layered style with textured ends and loose waves, often sun-lightened. It is meant to look natural and windswept rather than groomed.","It grew out of California surf culture in the 1950s and 1960s, and is now a common casual look. No clinical research addresses it."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People grow out layers and style with sea-salt spray, texturising products and air-drying, with occasional trims. It suits straight, wavy and curly textures."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["No health risks; frequent salt sprays and sun may dry hair, and the look needs enough hair density and length to carry."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Surfer_hair.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Surfer_hair.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Surfer hair","license":"CC BY 2.0","creator":"Rachel Amarette","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surfer_hair.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Japanese_surfer_hair.jpg/960px-Japanese_surfer_hair.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/Japanese_surfer_hair.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Japanese surfer hair","license":"CC BY 2.0","creator":"Jim Epler","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_surfer_hair.jpg","source":"Wik