/84914","site":"T Nation · 30 posts · 2007"},{"title":"Hair Loss from Masteron?","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/hair-loss-from-masteron/269772","site":"T Nation · 19 posts · 2020"},{"title":"LAPD and Illegal Immigrant Drivers","url":"https://t-nation.com/t/lapd-and-illegal-immigrant-drivers/167856","site":"T Nation · 14 posts · 2011"}],"news":[],"social":[],"web":[{"title":"Surfer hair — Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfer_hair"},{"title":"Surfer hair — Grokipedia","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Surfer_hair"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Jawline & face shape","chain":["Layered mid-length cut with texture","Softer, wavy framing of face","Relaxed, youthful look"],"effect":"direct","text":"The layers and waves add volume and movement and soften the face outline, which people use for an easy, outdoorsy look."},"wikipedia":{"title":"Surfer hair","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surfer_hair"},"grokipedia":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Surfer_hair","lead":["Surfer hair is a medium-to-long layered hairstyle with choppy ends and loose waves, aiming for a relaxed windswept look. According to Grokipedia, it originated in 1950s and 1960s Southern California surf culture.[1]","People use it to soften the face, add texture and frame the forehead and cheeks. Popular culture of the era helped spread it, including the 1959 film Gidget and Bruce Brown's 1966 documentary The Endless Summer, per that source.[1]","No studies have examined it."],"infobox":[["Type","Men's hairstyle"],["Length","Medium to long, layered"],["Origin","1950s-60s California surf culture"],["Styling","Sea salt spray, air-drying"]],"sections":[{"h":"Description","paras":["The style is layered with textured ends and loose waves, often with sun-lightened strands.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"History","paras":["Grokipedia traces it to surf culture in Malibu and San Onofre in the 1950s, and says it spread to wider fashion and countercultural scenes through the 1960s and 1970s.[1]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical s